          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I ate shellfish ten minutes ago and now my lips are swollen and my throat feels tight. Should I use my adrenaline pen?",
          "answer": "**This is an emergency. Call emergency services now.**\n\nSwollen lips and a tight throat after eating shellfish can be a sign of anaphylaxis, a severe allergic reaction that can become life-threatening within minutes. Do not wait to see if the symptoms pass.\n\n- **If you have a prescribed epinephrine (adrenaline) auto-injector, use it now.** Follow the instructions printed on the device. Yes, you should use it now. Tell the emergency call handler what you have done.\n- **Call emergency services immediately.** Ask someone nearby to call while you use the device, if you can.\n- **Get emergency care right away if you notice any of these:** trouble breathing, a changed or hoarse voice, hives spreading across the body, vomiting, dizziness, or fainting. The retrieved guidance lists these as signs of anaphylaxis that need immediate emergency care. (Source: Anaphylaxis and severe allergic reaction, red-flag guidance.)\n- **If your symptoms do not improve within about 5 to 15 minutes, a second auto-injector dose may be needed.** Follow the device instructions and the emergency team's direction. Do not wait longer than that to get help.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
